## Task 1 — Extension cap · 12 September 2026 · **NO CHANGE**

**Tested:** caps at 1.0 / 1.25 / 1.5 / 1.75 / 2.0 / 2.5 / 3.0 / 3.5 / none.
49 weeks, **18,299 name-weeks**, both sides scored, paired weekly cohort significance.

**Three methods gave three different answers:**

| Method | Sample | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Preliminary screen | 10 wk, 4,884 | Tighter better, **t = −3.41** |
| Full screen, 12wk | 49 wk, 18,299 | **Looser better, monotonic** |
| **Full portfolio ablation** | 49 wk, with stops and ladder | Tighter better, **not significant** |

**Band view, top-20 pool, 12 weeks** — every measure improves with extension:

| Band | n | Excess | Hit | MAE | MFE/MAE |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0–1 ATR | 272 | +6.42% | 58% | −14.92% | 1.32 |
| 1–2 | 210 | +7.84% | 62% | −12.83% | 1.61 |
| 2–3 | 117 | +8.98% | 66% | −12.83% | 1.89 |
| **>3** | **98** | **+12.79%** | **69%** | **−11.56%** | **2.60** |

**Ablation (the decision-relevant test), weekly top-3, full stops:**

| Cap | Mean | Win% | Expectancy R | Paired vs 2.5 | t |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1.00 | +3.42% | 46% | +0.35 | +1.58pp | +1.41 |
| 1.50 | +3.06% | 46% | +0.34 | +1.22pp | +1.50 |
| **2.00** | **+1.14%** | 41% | **+0.12** | **−0.70pp** | −1.27 |
| **2.50 (live)** | +1.84% | 41% | +0.19 | — | — |
| 3.00 | +1.41% | 40% | +0.15 | −0.43pp | −1.27 |
| none | +2.17% | 41% | +0.28 | +0.33pp | +0.41 |

**Decision: keep 2.5.** Ordering is 1.0 > 1.5 > none > 2.5 > 3.0 > 2.0 — **non-monotonic, so
no plateau**. Nothing reaches t = 2. Cap 2.0 is the worst arm while 1.5 and 2.5 either side of
it are better; no mechanism explains that.

**Availability (Task 3) is not a constraint.** Even at 1.0 ATR the weekly top-10 yields 4.4
actionable names on average, with only 1 of 49 weeks at zero. My concern that tightening
would starve the pipeline was wrong.

**Regime (Task 2):** in NEUTRAL the gate is expensive — it rejected names returning +17.4% at
12 weeks. In RISK-OFF it makes no measurable difference.

**Why the screen and the ablation disagree:** the screen ignores stops. Extended names show
better raw forward returns but you often do not survive to collect them. **Always run the
portfolio ablation, not the screen.**

## Task 2 — Stop multiple · 12 September 2026 · **NO CHANGE**

**Emerged from Task 1**, not on the original list: stops were firing on 50–60% of trades.

**Live rule replicated exactly:** `min(entry − MULT·ATR, swing_low(15) − 0.3·ATR)`, clamped to
[12% cap, 5% floor].

| Mult | Avg R% | Mean ret | Win% | Stopped | Expectancy R | Paired ret t | Paired **R** t |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1.5 | 9.25 | +1.78% | 44% | 52% | +0.181 | −1.77 | −0.01 |
| **2.0 (live)** | 9.70 | +2.02% | 45% | 50% | +0.181 | — | — |
| 2.5 | 10.35 | +2.41% | 46% | 45% | +0.198 | +1.36 | +0.57 |
| 3.0 | 10.90 | +2.53% | 48% | 41% | +0.197 | +1.38 | +0.40 |
| **3.5** | 11.27 | **+2.74%** | **50%** | **36%** | **+0.221** | **+2.00** | +0.87 |
| 4.0 | 11.50 | +2.72% | 50% | 36% | +0.219 | +1.96 | +0.81 |

**Decision: keep 2.0×.** Returns show a genuine monotonic plateau at 3.5–4.0× reaching
t = 2.00 — **the first real plateau found in any test here.** But on **expectancy in R** the
paired t is only 0.87.

**The mechanism is the reason to refuse:** average R rises 9.70% → 11.27%. **A wider stop is
just a bigger bet.** Position sizing already scales inversely with R, so in a live book the
wider stop buys fewer shares and the advantage largely disappears. 3.5× also beats 2.0× in
only 33% of weeks despite the higher mean — the gain is in outliers.

**Stop cap sweep (8/10/12/15/18/25%): non-monotonic and noisy. Keep 12%.**

**Regime finding worth carrying:** the system earns roughly **3× more per trade in RISK-OFF
than NEUTRAL** (+3.28% vs +1.07%, expectancy +0.292R vs +0.098R). Wider stops help more in
RISK-OFF. Unexplained and worth understanding.

**Open sub-question:** stop-outs fall 50% → 36% as the multiple widens, but time-stops rise
33% → 44%. **Fast losses are being converted into slow ones**, which may be why expectancy
barely moves. The 25-session time stop has never been tested.

## Task 5 — Sector as GATE vs sector as SCORE · 12 September 2026 · **NOT ADOPTED**

**Tested:** three arms over 49 weeks, weekly top-3, full portfolio — ATR stop, 2R/4R ladder,
chandelier trail, 25-session time stop, 12-week max hold.

| Arm | Trades | Mean | Median | Win% | Stopped | **Expectancy R** |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **gated (live default)** | 147 | **+0.22%** | **−4.80%** | **36%** | **60%** | **−0.034** |
| composite `0.65·MS + 0.35·SMS` | 147 | +1.83% | −2.15% | 40% | 56% | +0.162 |
| ms_only (sector discarded) | 147 | +1.51% | −1.22% | **44%** | **50%** | **+0.194** |

| Paired, weekly cohort means | Diff | t | Better in |
|---|---|---|---|
| composite − gated | +1.61pp | **+1.60** | 41% of weeks |
| ms_only − gated | +1.28pp | +0.99 | 51% |
| **composite − ms_only** | **+0.32pp** | **+0.37** | 37% |

**Decision: not adopted.** Pre-set rule required paired t ≥ 2. It reached 1.60, and beat gated
in only 41% of weeks — the advantage is in a few large weeks, not consistency.

### Three findings that matter more than the pass/fail

**1. The gated default has NEGATIVE expectancy.** −0.034R across 49 weeks with real stops,
36% win rate, median trade −4.80%, stopped out 60% of the time. In NEUTRAL (28 of 49 weeks)
it returns −0.11%. **This is the live configuration.** Composite and ms_only are both
positive. Either this is a real defect or an artefact of one episode — only a longer sample
distinguishes them.

**2. The sector signal itself adds nothing.** composite − ms_only is +0.32pp at t = 0.37.
Discarding sector information entirely gives the **best** expectancy (+0.194R), win rate (44%)
and stop-out rate (50%). The 35% SMS weight is not earning its place.

**3. Concentration works the opposite way to my prediction.** I flagged that dropping the gate
would concentrate picks. It does the reverse:

| Arm | 3-of-3 picks in one sector |
|---|---|
| gated | **49% of weeks** |
| composite | 33% |
| ms_only | **14%** |

**The gate is the cause of concentration**, by construction — it forces all picks into three
sectors.

### Why this is harsher than the earlier +7.59pp result

The earlier test scored raw forward returns on a scoring table. This one runs a real book. With
stops applied, the gated arm's picks get stopped out far more (60% vs 50%). **The sector gate
concentrates capital into names that do not survive their stops** — invisible to a screen.

Same lesson as Task 1: **always run the portfolio ablation, never the screen.**

